  "aiQBath": "How often should I bathe my dog?",
  "aiQFeeding": "How much should I feed my pet?",
  "aiQToxicFood": "Which human foods are unsafe for pets?",
  "aiQVaccines": "Which vaccines does my pet need?",
  "aiQDeworming": "How often should I deworm?",
  "aiQTicks": "How do I prevent ticks and fleas?",
  "aiQHeat": "How do I keep my pet cool in summer?",
  "aiQVomiting": "My pet vomited. What should I do?",
  "aiQDiarrhoea": "My pet has loose motions. What should I do?",
  "aiQNotEating": "My pet is not eating. Should I worry?",
  "aiQItching": "Why does my dog keep scratching?",
  "aiQLimping": "My dog is limping. What should I do?",
  "aiQLitter": "My cat isn't using the litter box. Why?",
  "aiQChewing": "How do I stop my puppy chewing and biting?",
  "aiQNails": "How do I trim my pet's nails?",
  "aiQTeeth": "How do I look after my pet's teeth?",
  "aiQBirdDiet": "What should I feed my bird?",
  "aiQRabbitDiet": "What should I feed my rabbit?",
  "aiQWeight": "Is my pet overweight?",
  "aiQAnxiety": "My pet is scared of crackers and thunder. How can I help?",
  "aiABath": "Most dogs need a bath every 3 to 4 weeks; more often only if they get dirty or smelly. Use a dog shampoo (human shampoo can dry the skin), lukewarm water, and rinse well. Dry the ears and the coat fully, especially in the monsoon, to avoid fungal infections. Brushing a few times a week keeps the coat clean between baths. Cats rarely need baths.",
  "aiAFeeding": "It depends on your pet's weight, age and activity. Start with the amount on the food pack for your pet's ideal weight, split into 2 meals for adult dogs and cats (3 to 4 for puppies and kittens). Check the waist and ribs every few weeks: you should feel the ribs easily. Treats should be under a tenth of daily food. Keep fresh water out all the time. Your vet can suggest an exact amount.",
  "aiAToxicFood": "Never give chocolate, grapes or raisins, onion, garlic, xylitol (in some sugar-free gum and sweets), alcohol, caffeine, cooked bones, or very salty and spicy food. Many festival sweets contain chocolate, raisins or xylitol, so keep them out of reach. Cats are also harmed by lilies. If your pet has eaten any of these, use SOS or call a vet now, even if they seem fine.",
  "aiAVaccines": "Dogs need DHPPi with Leptospirosis (3 puppy doses from 6 to 8 weeks), anti-rabies at about 3 months, and yearly boosters. Cats need the tricat vaccine and anti-rabies, also with yearly boosters. Rabbits and birds usually don't need routine vaccines in India. Your pet's Health tab shows the full schedule, what's due and what's overdue. Your vet decides the final plan.",
  "aiADeworming": "Puppies and kittens: every 2 weeks until 3 months old, then monthly until 6 months. Adults: every 3 months. Weigh your pet before each dose, because the dose depends on weight. PawSetu reminds you when the next dose is due. Ask your vet which dewormer suits your pet.",
  "aiATicks": "Use a vet-recommended tick and flea preventive all year: a spot-on, a chewable tablet or a collar. Ticks are common in India, especially in the monsoon. Check ears, between the toes and under the collar after walks. Keep bedding clean and dry. Never use dog products on cats. If your pet is weak, has pale gums or a fever after tick bites, see a vet quickly.",
  "aiAHeat": "Walk early in the morning or after sunset, and test the road with your hand: if it's too hot for 5 seconds, it's too hot for paws. Keep cool water and shade available all day. Never leave a pet in a parked car. Flat-faced breeds like pugs and Persian cats overheat fastest. Heavy panting, drooling, wobbling or bright red gums are signs of heatstroke: use SOS.",
  "aiAVomiting": "One vomit with a normal, playful pet often settles: remove food for a few hours (not water), then give small, bland meals like boiled rice with boiled chicken. See a vet the same day if vomiting repeats, there is blood, your pet is a puppy or kitten, seems weak, has a swollen belly, or may have eaten something toxic or a toy.",
  "aiADiarrhoea": "Keep fresh water available so your pet doesn't get dehydrated, and give small bland meals (boiled rice with boiled chicken or curd rice for dogs) for a day or two. Avoid milk and treats. See a vet if it lasts more than a day, there is blood or black stool, your pet is vomiting too, seems weak, or is a puppy or kitten.",
  "aiANotEating": "Skipping one meal can happen with heat, stress or a change in food. Offer fresh food and water and watch for other signs. See a vet if a dog doesn't eat for more than a day, a cat for more than 24 hours, a rabbit for even a few hours (rabbits get sick quickly), or if there is vomiting, weakness, pain or a fever.",
  "aiAItching": "Common causes are fleas and ticks, skin infections (often in the monsoon), food or dust allergies, and dry skin from frequent baths. Check the skin for ticks, redness, bald patches or a smell. Keep up tick and flea prevention and dry your dog well after baths and rain. If scratching goes on for more than a few days or the skin is broken, see a vet.",
  "aiALimping": "Check the paw for thorns, glass, cuts, a broken nail or a tick between the toes, and look for swelling. Rest your dog: short toilet walks only, no running or jumping, for a day. Never give human painkillers; many are poisonous to pets. See a vet if the limp lasts more than a day, your dog can't put weight on the leg, cries in pain, or after a fall or accident.",
  "aiALitter": "Keep the box clean (scoop daily), in a quiet spot away from food, with one box per cat plus one extra. Some cats dislike scented litter or covered boxes. Stress, like a new pet or a move, can also cause it. A cat straining in the box with little or no urine is an emergency, especially a male cat: use SOS.",
  "aiAChewing": "Puppies explore with their mouths and chew more while teething (3 to 6 months). Give safe chew toys and swap them in when your puppy grabs something else. If they nip, stop play for a few seconds, then continue gently. Praise calm play. Keep wires, shoes and medicines out of reach. Plenty of walks and play reduce chewing. A trainer can help if it continues after 6 months.",
  "aiANails": "Trim every 3 to 4 weeks, or when you hear nails clicking on the floor. Use pet nail clippers and cut only the tip, below the pink quick (on dark nails, take tiny slices). Keep styptic powder or plain cornflour ready in case of bleeding. Reward with a treat after each paw. If your pet hates it, a groomer or vet can do it in minutes. Birds and rabbits need nail trims too.",
  "aiATeeth": "Brush your dog's or cat's teeth 2 to 3 times a week with a pet toothbrush and pet toothpaste (human toothpaste is not safe to swallow). Start slowly with a finger brush and lots of praise. Dental chews help a little. Bad breath, red gums, a broken tooth or dropping food can mean dental disease: book a dental check with your vet.",
  "aiABirdDiet": "Seeds alone are not enough. Cockatiels and budgies do best on good-quality pellets with fresh vegetables daily (spinach, carrot, beans, peas) and small amounts of fruit, plus a little seed as a treat. Give fresh water every day. Never give avocado, chocolate, caffeine, salt or onion. Many parrot species are protected in India; only keep legally allowed birds.",
  "aiARabbitDiet": "Hay should be about 80% of a rabbit's diet, available all day: it keeps teeth and gut healthy. Add a handful of leafy greens (coriander, mint, lettuce, carrot tops) daily, a small amount of rabbit pellets, and fruit only as a rare treat. Always keep fresh water. A rabbit that stops eating or pooping for even a few hours needs a vet urgently.",
  "aiAWeight": "Feel the ribs: you should feel them easily under a thin layer, and see a waist from above. If you can't, your pet may be overweight. Weigh your pet monthly in PawSetu to see the trend against the healthy range for the breed. Cut treats and table scraps, measure food, and add a little more walking or play. Ask your vet before a big diet change.",
  "aiAAnxiety": "Before Diwali or a storm, make a quiet den: a covered crate or a corner with their bed, curtains closed, a fan or soft music on. Walk your dog early in the day and keep them indoors with ID tags on when crackers start. Stay calm and close, but don't force cuddles. Don't scold fear. For severe fear, ask your vet well in advance about calming options.",
